[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — then let's decompose. Here's how I'd split it:

  1. [1mTasks core[0m — task model, persistence, list/create/edit UI. The thing notifications are [3mabout[0m.
  2. [1mIdentity & sharing[0m — users, and how a task becomes visible or relevant to more than one person.
  3. [1mChange events[0m — a durable record of [3mwhat changed, when, by whom[0m. Notifications read from this; an activity feed and
     an audit log also fall out of it.
  4. [1mNotifications[0m — subscriptions ("tasks I care about"), delivery channels, read/unread state.

  Each gets its own spec → plan → implementation cycle. Notifications is #4 because it's a consumer of the other three —
  it has almost no design surface of its own until they exist.

  I'll brainstorm [1m#1, the tasks core[0m, now. But one question upstream of it shapes everything, including whether #2 and
  #3 are even real.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this is a single-person tool or a shared one. It decides the whole stack, and it's the expensive
  thing to change later.

  - [1mSingle-user, browser-only[0m — tasks in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, no server, no accounts, deploys as static files. Cheapest by far.
    But it makes notifications near-meaningless: if you're the only one who changes anything, there's nothing to be
    told. "Notifications" would collapse into due-date reminders.
